{"agent":"fix-reviewid","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813-Godzila","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The new fallback assumes the first tied reviewer (`best`) is the author’s family and excludes only `best.family`. When no `[agent].family` or session model is set and `review_tier` returns `\"none\"` for every reviewer, reversing the reviewer order makes it wrong: e.g. `xai` listed before `deepseek` gives `best.family == \"xai\"`; the loop then returns the first non-`xai` tied reviewer, which is the same-family `deepseek` reviewer. `complete --model deepseek` can still refuse “reviewer independence not satisfied.” The added test only covers `deepseek` listed first, so the fix is order-dependent rather than resolving the unknown-author identifying-reviewer selection generally.","digest":"38190b4214a29da8","location":"ddflow/api/review/run.py:_identifying_reviewer fallback","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/api/review/run.py:_identifying_reviewer fallback"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"When the author cannot be named, `review_tier` yields no cross-family hit, and the new branch treats the tied-first reviewer’s family as the author’s and returns the next family instead. That is only true if config order lists the lane model first. Given an unknown author and clean tied results ordered grok/xai, gemini/google, deepseek, this returns gemini. `complete --model` for a google-family author then fails reviewer independence, even though the previous `return best` path would have named grok (cross-family) and passed. The new test only places deepseek first, so it never sees this inversion.","digest":"eedb8c179499e6e7","location":"ddflow/api/review/run.py:416","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/review/run.py:416"}],"chunks_total":1,"combined_gates":["rubber_duck","critic"],"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":4730,"diff_sha":"5096e8fbaa5114386070d5542595aa58e42c9d15c2105d151bd3bce883a4f86f","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracke